Saved error-output state during variable binding
================================================

MBIND captures the current $ERRORMSG value for SYMBOL-VALUES-IN. Assignment
hooks may perform another binding before the outer invocation saves that
option, and concurrent invocations may overlap. A shared SETQ lets either
call overwrite the other's saved value, so MUNBIND restores the wrong option.
MBIND now dynamically binds *$ERRORMSG-VALUE* for its own invocation.

The existing capture-at-entry behavior, assignment validation, binding and
unwinding bodies are preserved. WITH-$ERROR currently changes the condition-
signalling flag; older comments saying it also binds $ERRORMSG to NIL were
stale and have been corrected. This scratch-state fix does not isolate the
separate shared value cells of arbitrary BLOCK locals or function parameters.

Run with a timeout and --no-init:

  ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_mset"]);'

Require the completed rtest_mset suite summary. A Lisp error can exit zero. The 30 appended examples preserve all previous problem numbers
and mathematical expectations. MBIND-ERROR-CHECK.LISP uses the existing
PARALLEL-INPUT-CHECK.LISP execution-site helper.

Checks cover true/false options, nested assignment hooks, empty bindings,
multiple return values, controlled body errors and nonlocal exits, parameter
count errors, rejected assignments before/after partial progress, and binding
and call-stack restoration. A fixed private generator varies execution site,
option, nesting depth and exit mode over 64 cases. Temporary symbols and
fixture-local option registries avoid the separate variable-cell defect.

Four native handshakes pause one binding before it saves $ERRORMSG until a
second worker binds with the opposite setting. They cover both a completed
second binding and two active bindings unwinding in the opposite order. Both saved and
restored values must belong to the first invocation. Deadlines, cancellation
and worker joins bound the checks; cleanup restores the shared scratch cell
when running the deliberately faulty parent. CLISP checks portable fallback.

The initially unbound option check requires the saved MUNBOUND markers,
scratch restoration and preservation of the enclosing option value. It calls
MBIND directly inside a PROGV fixture, which owns and unwinds the temporary
values. It deliberately does not call MUNBIND on the initially unbound option:
ECL 21.2.1's MAKUNBOUND removes the global cell while leaving a PROGV binding
bound. This was reproduced without Maxima; a test using it on $ERRORMSG leaked
state into later tests. The fixture now checks the enclosing BOUNDP/value as
well. Ordinary bound-variable and error cleanup remain covered by the other
checks. Maxima's compatibility helper and the separate current-binding
removal tests are described in README.unbinding.
